==English==

===Etymology===
From late 17th century {{etyl|fr|en}} {{m|fr|effronterie|}}, from {{m|fr|effronté||shameless, insolent}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|esfronté|}}, from {{etyl|VL.|en}} {{m|la|*exfrontātus}}. Compare {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|effrōns||barefaced}}, from the prefix {{m|la|ex-||from}} + {{m|la|frōns||forehead}} (English: {{l|en|front}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪˈfɹʌntəɹi/|/ɛˈfɹʌntəɹi/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[insolent|Insolent]] and [[shameless]] [[audacity]].
#: {{ux|en|We even had the '''effrontery''' to suggest that he should leave the country.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} An [[act]] of insolent and shameless audacity.
#: {{ux|en|Any refusal to salute the president shall be counted as an '''effrontery'''.}}

